{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Editorial — Brilliant.org / Quanta Magazine\n      Soft, narrative, illustrated. Reads like a beautifully designed essay.\n    \n  \n  \n    \n    \n    \n    chainlink-ledger.local/btc/transactions\n  \n  \n    Bitcoin · Module 2 of 9\n    What moves through Bitcoin?\n    Imagine you slide a $20 bill across a table. The bill itself doesn't change — only who holds it. Bitcoin works the same way, but the “bill” is a chain of digital signatures, and the table is a public ledger anyone can audit.\n    The unit of movement is called a transaction. It is the only thing Bitcoin actually stores. Blocks just bundle them.\n    \n      \n        \n          \n            \n          \n        \n        \n        Alice\n        5.0 BTC (input)\n        \n        \n        Transaction\n        signed by Alice\n        → Bob 4.99 BTC\n        → fee 0.01 BTC\n        \n        \n        Bob\n        4.99 BTC\n      \n    \n    \n      Check yourself\n      Where does the 0.01 BTC fee go?\n      ⚪   Burned forever\n      ⚪   Paid to whoever mines the block this lands in\n      ⚪   Returned to Alice if Bob doesn't claim within 10 minutes","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1778435788903}
